### 6. Data Modelling

After cleaning the datasets, I created relationships between the tables in Power BI.

The model connects customers, accounts, branches, transactions, loans, loan payments, cards, and the Date Table.

This allows information from different banking areas to be analysed together and supports interactive filtering across the dashboards.

### 7. KPI Definitions

KPIs (Key Performance Indicators) are measurements used to track and evaluate business performance.

| KPI | Definition |
|---|---|
| Total Customers | Count of unique customer IDs |
| Total Accounts | Count of account records |
| Active Accounts | Count of accounts with Active status |
| Active Account % | (Active Accounts / Total Accounts) × 100 |
| Total Branches | Count of unique branch IDs |
| Total Transactions | Count of transaction records |
| Total Transaction Amount | Sum of transaction amounts |
| Average Transaction Amount | Total Transaction Amount / Total Transactions |
| Total Loan Amount | Sum of loan amounts |
| Outstanding Loan Balance | Sum of outstanding loan balances |
| Total Loan Payments | Count of loan payment records |
| Delayed Payment Records | Count of payment records where Days Late is greater than zero |
| Delayed Payment % | (Delayed Payment Records / Total Loan Payments) × 100 |
| Total Cards | Count of card records |
| Active Cards | Count of cards with Active status |
| Card Activation % | (Active Cards / Total Cards) × 100 |

*Note: These are KPI calculation definitions. Actual DAX measures should use the corresponding column names and filters in the Power BI model.*

